From ef1805bb4bf1b7b6656f42b68c2a07896d2745d6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Carlos Augusto <mb@lab.how>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 14:08:24 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] [KV Connector] Serve external prefix hits for align-mode
 Mamba hybrids

With the complex OffloadingConnector enabled for a hybrid model that has
align-mode Mamba groups (Qwen3.8-Flash-Next: 50 KV cache groups, 13 of them
GDN recurrent state), multi-turn serving corrupts deterministically at
temp=0: the cold turn (cached_tokens=0) answers correctly; every later turn
with a prefix hit answers a generic greeting or nothing, with no assert and
a healthy engine. The model reads its own context as empty. Inert under
SimpleCPUOffloadConnector, which never stores Mamba groups.

Two independent defects on the external-hit path:

1. Stale boundary capture. Align-mode Mamba migrates its state column once
   per step, pre-forward. A chunked-prefill step computes an entire chunk at
   once (e.g. tokens 6016..9101 in the step starting at nct=6016), so the
   column ends the step holding the chunk-END state; the intermediate
   retention-boundary state (9024) never exists in any column. The
   connector's deferred store DMA faithfully copied the chunk-end state and
   filed it under the boundary key. On resume, the request recomputed the
   chunk tail on top of a state that already included it: recurrent-state
   double-processing.

2. Untransported padded tail. QSA attention groups pad their pages; the
   compressed-K selector tail is the [unpadded, padded) region (192,512 B
   here), read by selection through compressed_block_table. Narrow transfer
   refs carried the unpadded page size, so that region was never DMA'd in
   either direction: a resumed request selected against an empty tail while
   every copied byte looked correct.

Fix:
- Boundary stores source dedicated capture blocks, written once by the
  boundary-checkpoint crossing copy and manager-pinned from offer until store
  ack or drop; captures recorded after their producing step completed are
  withheld. Pins release on every store drop path (out-of-bounds, allocation
  failure, key dedup, stale-ack drain with live-fence guard), and
  block-multiple geometry is asserted at record and config sites.
- Attention transfer refs carry the full padded page
  (spec.page_size_bytes); store and load share one size source; canonical ref
  width comes from each layer's own group spec (a stale loop variable made
  the padded-tail transport silently no-op on hybrid layouts whose last
  group is Mamba); Mamba refs keep the unpadded width.
- The QSA selector pool fresh flag is derived from the restored-checkpoint
  signal at add time and cleared on boundary
  (get_recurrent_checkpoint_fresh); the anchor seeds to prefix-1. GLM-Next
  mirrors the gating. Local hits and non-offload paths keep stock
  always-fresh behavior.
- The external hit extends to the deepest boundary every group keys, never a
  half-restore.

Tests ship with the patch: crossing-capture digest identity, pin release on
store-ack and every offer-drop path, hit-gate convergence, padded-ref
register width per backend plus a GPU<->CPU DMA roundtrip, QSA fresh gating
on cold/resume/local-hit adds, store-key dedup, and the b12x GDN prefill
checkpoint clone.

Live serving validation (drizzle, RTX PRO 6000, temp=0, seed4+fixed nonce,
GPU cache capped at ~49,955 tokens so a resend of eight ~10.4k-token prompts
cannot fit in GPU): regular phase 9/9 correct, resume sends
cached_tokens=9024, corrupted=0; evict phase serving=8/8 correct=8/8 with
all B-resends at cached=9024 from the CPU tier. Pre-patch on the same model
and config: the first store of a large prompt crashed the engine (fixed by
the CoW alignment commit), and for requests that did complete stores, every
prefix-hit turn served a deterministic wrong answer.

Depends on: the block-tracking commit in this series, a port of
vllm-project/vllm#46960 (external-hit serving assumes it for MultiConnector
zero-token allocations).
